his", -0.9660670161247253]]}, {"t": " can", "lp": -0.0639650821685791, "top": [[" can", -0.0639650821685791], [" could", -2.790630578994751]]}, {"t": " verify", "lp": -0.12306292355060577, "top": [[" verify", -0.12306292355060577], [" confirm", -2.1597392559051514]]}, {"t": " the", "lp": -0.73004549741745, "top": [[" the", -0.73004549741745], [" these", -1.1837389469146729]]}, {"t": " precise", "lp": -0.3827877938747406, "top": [[" precise", -0.3827877938747406], [" exact", -1.1600438356399536]]}, {"t": " behavior", "lp": -0.7934461832046509, "top": [[" behavior", -0.7934461832046509], [" definitions", -1.150704026222229]]}, {"t": " of", "lp": -0.8245920538902283, "top": [[" of", -0.8245920538902283], [" for", -0.8355307579040527]]}, {"t": " each", "lp": -0.21769104897975922, "top": [[" each", -0.21769104897975922], [" these", -1.674798607826233]]}, {"t": " mode", "lp": -0.04065464064478874, "top": [[" mode", -0.04065464064478874], [" `", -3.5171122550964355]]}, {"t": "?", "lp": -0.24554593861103058, "top": [["?", -0.24554593861103058], [" for", -1.618543267250061]]}, {"t": "", "lp": -0.5748636722564697, "top": [["", -0.5748636722564697], [" That", -0.9947645664215088]]}], "text": "# CCS Mode on Intel Arc (xe driver)\n\nThe `ccs_mode` parameter controls how Compute Slice (CCS) contexts are distributed across the GPU's compute units. This is set via the `xe` kernel module parameter (e.g., `modprobe xe ccs_mode=1`).\n\n## The Modes\n\n**ccs_mode 0 (default)**\n- Uses the default heuristic for CCS distribution\n- The kernel picks CCS units automatically based on its own logic\n\n**ccs_mode 1**\n- Distributes CCS contexts across **all available CCS units**\n- Maximizes parallelism by spreading work across every compute slice\n- Best for workloads that benefit from broad distribution\n\n**ccs_mode 2**\n- Concentrates CCS usage on a **subset** of compute units\n- Can reduce contention and improve locality for certain patterns\n- May help when you want to isolate work or avoid specific hardware quirks\n\n## When to Use Mode 4\n\nMode 4 is typically used for **debugging or testing** purposes. It often forces a specific/deterministic CCS assignment pattern that makes it easier to reproduce issues or verify behavior. You'd reach for it when:\n- Reproducing a bug related to CCS scheduling\n- Testing driver behavior under controlled conditions\n- Diagnostics rather than production performance\n\n---\n\n**Important caveat:** I'm not fully certain about the exact semantics of each numeric mode, and these details can vary between driver versions. The Intel Xe driver documentation and the kernel source (`drivers/gpu/driver/xe/`) is the authoritative source.\n\nWould you like me to point you toward the specific documentation or source files where you can verify the precise behavior of each mode?"}